{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/db7d2b33f2badad28b754d4482047a33bb37469ed8b7b17859b10552840e2f51.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,4)$, \n$(-6,12)$ and $(-1,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/2e1dde461c6244a59672fc28c842b70a30297ac7a8c7e7cf2095a973cd1860cd.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/461ff9fb5ef66239eb81314f761fac9ceb32b3bc88df6940b61445c752fe3d0c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/bf7cc4e728cd1ade741c55b2fbb54720220e0e09a8521a150300532b4e3ee9ed.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/da61a7fd99ce683be4e66d3708ddf15c969b038f0250dc4c024ace870c3f7544.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/ed8a2354f8cc04f998875f599a52b50e6c64cafe8976f1bea176772543e295ac.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/771f1ffeda2b0041e1485611315661eed0bc88a601513d4fd4b2088022521aa8.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(1,-1)$,  and $(-2,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/e1faae8b1750b1c402f88ef801cd6609d259c37c523ac4bf4a7cb795034a9bd1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd"} {"question" "q-147523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/130f215103a0c955d14ac386a382afae09c22f507491bc54a408ad13a154cbc5.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/6b0143a0adc22d1834fc404c9da0d03b2143c8cbc16a29a9286637920694002d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/dc6d39b05ae4862121b7b76b555be7b19931eda478e84973181e74e8d628db2b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/e1faae8b1750b1c402f88ef801cd6609d259c37c523ac4bf4a7cb795034a9bd1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/f5be8864e70a021eb2fa717a9a7998c645e3792a68d23de54a07114b7a566cf1.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/e1faae8b1750b1c402f88ef801cd6609d259c37c523ac4bf4a7cb795034a9bd1.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,1),(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$  \n\n- $(6,-18)$\n- $(-5,-20)$\n- $(12,-24)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -24 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are  $(12,-24),(0,0)$ and $(-6,12).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd"} {"question" "q-147670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-147670" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/8978d3bb02b4032b6e3a8b4a85878db647eb8323ac5b0423b5a9a6fab121c7e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A point, different from the origin"] ["text" "A rectangle"] ["text" "A triangle"] ["text" "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A line segment"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0231cc5aaf661cc873f18ff676a03d66927be48b49d38649efc45860197d18c1.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cf444e4428213186917c4d85a01e25245d21b247f29d25a3832b05a20dac87fd"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A point, different from the origin"] ["text" "A quadrilateral"] ["text" "A triangle"] ["text" "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"} {"question" "q-162208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A quadrilateral"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/state.json" "file_sha256" "617cb9ce2aff3ec9573f3fbed3f2a2d65a6c92e1367aa9c97ce0738e5eb7d806"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1143 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
